# A missing hashed asset has to be a real 404, not the app shell: the old
# fallback answered a stale /assets/views-abc123.js with index.html and a 200,
# and the browser failed to parse HTML as a script. With code-split views that
# is the deploy-under-an-open-tab case (the router then shows "reload").
#
# Tested on a preview deploy (2026-10-18): Pages does not honour a 404 rule
# here — `/assets/*  /missing-asset.html  404` still returned index.html with a
# 200. What works is public/404.html: once it exists, Pages serves it with a 404
# for every path that is not a file, and that page refreshes a person on a
# stray path back to /. So the rule is kept only as a record:
# /assets/*  /404.html  404

# SPA fallback. Routing is hash-based, so deep links already resolve. With
# public/404.html present Pages answers unknown paths with that page instead
# (which sends people to /), so this line no longer changes anything; it is
# left so that removing 404.html restores the old behaviour.
/*    /index.html   200
